WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.073 --> 00:00:06.990
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:11.280
I'll admit gladly that I don't
like to drive, or cook, or get

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.280
groceries.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:13.320
I'm lazy.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.790
Sure, you can say that.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:16.830
But I know there
are millions of you

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.250
out there like me, who would
rather Uber than drive.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.600
Have you ever wondered how Uber
platform scales for millions

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.820
of concurrent users like us?

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.310
They must get billions of
database transactions a day.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:34.530
I am absolutely fascinated by
how Uber designed its platform

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.260
to handle those insane
number of requests.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.700
So I invited Ankit Srivastava,
senior staff engineer at Uber,

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.440
to learn all about it.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.310
Stick around, because this
ride is going to be awesome.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:48.210
Hi, Ankit.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.430
Thanks for joining
us on the show today.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:52.597
ANKIT SRIVASTAVA: Thank you
for having me, Priyanka.

00:00:52.597 --> 00:00:53.770
Really glad to be here.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:56.190
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So we
all know what Uber is,

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.940
but to kick-start
our conversation,

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.320
let's start with what powers
that simple experience

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.840
of clicking a button
to get a ride,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.423
or get food at our doorsteps.

00:01:05.423 --> 00:01:07.590
ANKIT SRIVASTAVA: You know,
Priyanka, the first time

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.530
I used Uber, I fell in love
with the simple experience

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.380
where I could get a car
at the tap of a button.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.910
It seemed magical.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.370
Being here at Uber for
the past six years,

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:19.380
and the pace at which
Uber is evolving

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.290
to become the essential for
day-to-day consumer needs,

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.080
I'm still learning all the
intricacies of various product

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.940
features that we have.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.280
At a very high level,
when a consumer clicks

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.320
on the Get A Ride
or Get Food button,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.930
we capture the user's
intent and fulfill it

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:37.260
by matching it with the right
set of providers nearby.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.990
Fulfillment models the
user's intent as demand,

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.270
and any active
provider session that

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.960
can fulfill this demand
as supply in its systems.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.550
We in the fulfillment
team at Uber

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.010
developed platforms to
orchestrate and manage

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.220
the lifecycle of ongoing
orders, jobs, and providers

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.700
across millions of active user
sessions around the world.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.460
In essence, fulfillment
is a foundational platform

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.120
capability that powers
existing lines of businesses

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.990
and enables rapid scaling
of new verticals seamlessly.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:10.080
The platform handles millions
of concurrent user sessions

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.740
and billions of trips per year,
across thousands of cities

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:14.760
globally.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.880
We handle billions of database
transactions every day.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.160
There are hundreds of
microservices at Uber

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.590
that rely on the platform
as a source of [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:27.120
for the accurate state of any
ongoing order, job, or a driver

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.910
and a delivery person session.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:31.290
Now, the events that are
generated by this platform,

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.240
they're used by
hundreds of offline data

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.780
sets to make critical
business decisions.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.830
500-plus developers extend the
platform using APIs, events,

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:44.370
and code, to build
120-plus unique fulfillment

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.892
flows across various
lines of businesses.

00:02:46.892 --> 00:02:47.850
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Wow.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:48.390
All right.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.820
So this fulfillment
platform-- it

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:54.210
sounds like this is the
backbone of Uber's architecture.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.610
Now, let's say I
ordered an Uber.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:03:00.600
How does the request flow work
from the driver, to the rider,

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.237
to this fulfillment platform?

00:03:02.237 --> 00:03:04.320
ANKIT SRIVASTAVA: When you
open the Uber rider app

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.300
and request a ride,
the request goes

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.310
through a series of
checks and validation,

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.710
and it eventually triggers
fulfillment to create

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.690
a new order for the consumer.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.300
This order represents the
intent of the consumer,

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.670
and we translate the
intent to individual jobs

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.120
that need to be processed.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.880
And we store all of this
information in Spanner.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.840
Now, these jobs are periodically
read by our matching engine,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.680
and offered to any open
provider or supplier nearby.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.960
Now, we used to store
all of this data

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.303
in our on-prem database, and
we recently moved to Spanner.

00:03:34.303 --> 00:03:35.220
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.530
So what were the
challenges you were

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.200
facing with this
on-prem database

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.760
in this original architecture?

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.010
ANKIT SRIVASTAVA: We leveraged
a NoSQL storage engine,

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.750
Cassandra, to
maintain the real time

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.810
state of all fulfillment
entities on prem.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.780
In order to maintain some
notion of serialized ability,

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.120
on top of Cassandra
we used Ringpop

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.750
to provide individual
entity-level serialization.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.700
There were a few
major challenges,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.760
but let me highlight
a couple of them.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.230
Cassandra is built
on the paradigm

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.640
of eventual consistency.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.100
In addition to that, in a
multi-data center setup,

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.400
it's really hard to guarantee
low-latency quorum writes

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:12.990
with Cassandra.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.120
As Uber's products
and services evolved,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.970
we started seeing complex
storage interactions

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:21.529
that required multi-row
and multi-table writes.

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:23.780
So we had to build an
application layer framework

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:27.610
to orchestrate this operation
by leveraging the saga pattern.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.040
With the underlying storage
platform at Cassandra

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.110
being eventually
consistent, it often

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:36.380
led to inconsistencies that
had to be manually corrected.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:39.080
What it translated in
the real world would be,

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:41.760
a rider seeing two different
drivers dispatched to them.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.270
Imagine you being out
there near your office,

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.990
and there are two drivers
who arrive to pick you up.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.870
It's a bit confusing
for anyone out there.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:54.110
As we scale globally to
millions of active users,

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:56.540
even a notion of
consistency became harder

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.340
to achieve at our
application level.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.290
In addition to all
of this, as we scale,

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:05.180
we started seeing horizontal
scaling bottlenecks, as well,

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.760
in the overall architecture,
and really, because of the way

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:11.210
we started with our
application layer with Ringpop.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.460
This is when we started
tracing our steps back

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.250
to the properties of a
traditional SQL-based storage

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:19.190
engine that provides
asset guarantees.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.260
We started defining a
transactional storage

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:23.270
engine that would
provide consistency

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:25.740
as the primary capability.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:28.080
One of the things that has
been in mainstream technology

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.780
was New SQL storage
engines that combine

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:33.270
the scalability of
a NoSQL architecture

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.490
with strong ACID guarantees.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:37.980
This is when we came across
Google Cloud Spanner.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:39.870
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Now, so that definitely

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:43.350
sounds like a lot
of trials, and then

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:45.660
figuring out what's working
and what's not working.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.730
Definitely, the
eventual consistency

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:51.930
was not the aspect that could
be lived with-- especially

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:53.080
with this scale.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.190
So what were the thought
processes and methodologies

00:05:56.190 --> 00:06:00.960
that you were exploring when
transitioning from NoSQL to New

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:01.920
SQL?

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.545
ANKIT SRIVASTAVA:
When we were thinking

00:06:03.545 --> 00:06:06.030
about the next chapter for
Uber's fulfillment platform,

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.360
the focus on consistency became
one of the primary evaluation

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.200
criteria, along with high
resiliency and availability.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:15.720
Based on our requirements
and various benchmarks,

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.502
we evaluated various
New SQL storage

00:06:18.502 --> 00:06:19.710
engines that were out there--

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:23.250
CockroachDB, FoundationDB,
and Cloud Spanner.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.890
Cloud Spanner provided all
the functional requirements,

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:28.230
it scaled horizontally
with our benchmarks,

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:31.470
and provided us with a managed
solution for cluster management

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:32.370
and maintenance.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.890
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Now, how
does the new fulfillment

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:37.800
architecture with Spanner work?

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.522
Can we dive into
the architecture?

00:06:41.522 --> 00:06:42.480
ANKIT SRIVASTAVA: Yeah.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.040
We leveraged Spanner's
North America multi-region

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:46.930
configuration as
the storage engine

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:49.030
for all fulfillment entities.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:50.560
Our fulfillment
service transcends

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.530
Uber's own operational regions,
which are in North America

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:56.980
as well, and they make a
sequence of network cores

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:58.780
across to Spanner,
which is deployed

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:02.680
in Google Cloud for every
transaction that a user makes.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:05.680
So for every user request,
or any system operation,

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:09.010
it results in a transaction
against one or more rows

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:11.950
and across one or more
tables in Cloud Spanner.

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:13.480
With Spanner we
are able to provide

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:15.370
a consistent view
of all our data

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:18.342
to internal as well
as external customers.

00:07:18.342 --> 00:07:20.050
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So
it sounds like Uber

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:23.350
leverages this
hybrid of on-premise

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:25.240
and Cloud Infrastructure.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.090
Now, knowing that keeping
latency to the minimum

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:31.480
is the biggest factor
for an app like Uber,

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.480
how do you set up the
networking architecture that

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.570
enabled that seamless and
lowest latency interaction

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:40.990
between this fulfillment
application layer on premise

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:44.543
and then the Cloud Spanner,
which is in Google Cloud?

00:07:44.543 --> 00:07:46.710
ANKIT SRIVASTAVA: Thanks
to Uber's global networking

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:49.710
infrastructure team and Google
Cloud platform's networking

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:53.310
team, we have built an extremely
resilient and highly reliable

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:56.700
network infrastructure to
support Uber's workload.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:58.920
In order to achieve this,
we separated the networking

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.590
infrastructure into
two major components--

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.300
the physical layer,
that consists

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:06.360
of the interconnections between
Uber and the Cloud vendors,

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.000
and a logical
layer, that consists

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.030
of virtual connections on
top of the physical layer

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:14.130
to achieve redundancy.

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:16.170
We built redundancy
across these layers

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:18.930
by having multiple
routers and local access

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:21.900
points at each
physical network route,

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:23.850
and an additional
layer of redundancy

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:26.280
with the logical
connections on top.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:29.490
We even leverage Google's
private Google access

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.520
throughout all of Google's
traffic, the Google APIs

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:35.490
through the Cloud
interconnect VLAN attachments.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:37.500
This reduces the need
of routing the traffic

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:39.390
through public
internet, and provided

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:41.640
additional reliability.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.440
To validate our
architecture, the teams

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.180
came up with a
benchmarking suite that

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.180
validates all the physical
and logical network routes

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:51.180
extensively, and we
find any inefficiencies

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.980
in this test set
up very quickly.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:55.020
This was extremely
critical for the success

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:56.980
of the overall project.

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:57.980
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.290
I can only imagine
how connectivity

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:04.550
and those benchmarking
tests must have been so

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:07.710
critical to make this happen.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:10.730
Now, you've been on Spanner
for quite some time.

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:13.050
How did the transition
to Spanner help?

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:14.660
What were the benefits?

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:16.160
ANKIT SRIVASTAVA:
You know Priyanka,

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:18.620
building a specialized
database is extremely complex,

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:21.410
and maintaining or
operating it is much harder.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:24.680
With consistency as a primary
feature provided by Spanner,

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:26.600
and it being a managed
solution, they're

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:28.910
able to leverage a ton of
storage functionalities

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:30.120
out of the box.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:32.030
This has given us
time back to focus

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:33.830
on building the
domain of fulfillment,

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:37.350
and provide our customers
with richer experiences.

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.770
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So
I cannot even imagine,

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:42.290
when you were migrating
to Cloud Spanner,

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:45.060
we were still ordering
food and rides.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:48.920
How did the team make sure that
the migration and switchover

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:51.650
were seamless so that
the end users could

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:53.627
have the same experience?

00:09:53.627 --> 00:09:55.460
ANKIT SRIVASTAVA: This
was extremely tricky.

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:57.860
One of the things that we
agreed upon from the beginning

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:00.770
was an internal
platform re-architecture

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:03.500
should not impact the
consumers in any way.

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:05.960
So we focused on building
the technology that

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:08.630
would seamlessly
migrate any ongoing user

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:10.520
session from our
old architecture

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:12.230
to the new Spanner-based
architecture.

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:14.090
Now, since the data
models and database

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:17.540
topology of our old platform
was significantly different

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:20.180
from the new architecture
that leveraged Spanner,

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:23.810
any kind of light migration
of data was ruled out.

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:27.140
Initially, given the fact that
most of this data is ephemeral

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:29.630
and continuously
changing every minute,

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:31.430
doing a light migration
through backups

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:33.980
will only result
in loss of data.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:36.080
So we built a system
that intercepts

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:38.630
a request from a user
session and [INAUDIBLE] them

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:41.570
to the world where the
user session started.

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:43.910
If there are any ongoing
orders for the session,

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:45.530
we do not migrate
the user session

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:47.510
until the order is completed.

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:50.360
For an open user session
with no active orders,

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:52.100
we switch them to
the new architecture

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:53.990
which is backed by Spanner.

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:56.360
Now, the team automated all
of this through tooling,

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:58.100
and we tested the
tooling rigorously

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:01.340
in our testing, staging,
and shadow environments.

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:03.140
We even set up
test cities, where

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:05.645
we simulated hundreds
of riders and drivers,

00:11:05.645 --> 00:11:07.730
and their behavior
in the real world,

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:11.150
to ensure we can observe the
impact of this migration.

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:12.980
And these migrations
were done by one city

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:15.440
at a time, in some
cases a batch of cities,

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:18.050
and it took more than
six months to complete.

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:20.210
Spreading this migration
over six months

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:23.210
also helped us to continuously
migrate business features

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:25.220
from the old stack
to the new stack.

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:27.260
There were 120-plus
business features

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:30.740
that were to be migrated from
the old stack to the new stack.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:34.760
So the overall technical
and execution perspective,

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:37.420
it was a very difficult
and challenging project

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:38.880
that we undertook as a team.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:40.338
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
You're literally

00:11:40.338 --> 00:11:44.720
changing, like, the tires of
the car while the car is going.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:46.190
So [LAUGHS].

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:50.990
Yeah, I can totally imagine
how those benchmarking tests

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:54.590
and those tooling would
have been a critical part

00:11:54.590 --> 00:11:56.570
of this entire migration.

00:11:56.570 --> 00:11:59.270
When you run planet-scale
architecture like this--

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:02.000
and I don't use the word
"planet scale" a lot, but here,

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:05.300
it aptly fits, because this
platform handles millions

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:09.620
of concurrent users and billions
of trips per month across over

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:11.270
10,000 cities--

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:15.680
which translates to billions
of database transactions a day.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:18.110
There is a lot happening.

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:21.650
Optimizing and operating
Spanner at that scale

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:25.160
must be crucial, because you
need to keep the costs low

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:26.690
and performance high.

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:29.420
So let's bring in Pritam
Shah, engineering director

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:32.000
for Cloud Spanner,
who helped Uber bring

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:33.570
this architecture to life.

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:34.250
Hi, Pritam.

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:35.720
So nice to have you.

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:37.640
Walk us through the
migration journey

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:41.130
and the challenges that come
with this scale of migration.

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:43.010
PRITAM SHAH: Thank
you for having me.

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:45.980
On the migration
planning front, there

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:49.740
are five things I can talk
about in terms of challenges.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:53.390
First, how do we
test at Uber scale,

00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:55.520
and how do we review
the experience

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:58.370
that a rider would have,
and the performance metrics,

00:12:58.370 --> 00:13:00.380
so that we know we are ready?

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:04.970
So designing and executing
this test is a real challenge.

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:08.390
Second is agreeing
on migration first

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:10.940
with acceptable
performance, and then

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:14.570
fine tuning for better cost
and performance optimization

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:16.350
was another challenge.

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:18.630
The third one would
be capacity planning.

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:21.980
So how do we right size,
and have the right amount

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:24.578
of capacity in Spanner?

00:13:24.578 --> 00:13:26.870
The fourth one, which is one
of the most critical ones,

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:30.270
is how do we do this
migration with zero downtime?

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:33.770
So doing a live migration
with no disruption

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:36.680
to Uber's business is
quite a bit of challenge,

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:39.650
and how do we do it in phases,
while keeping the lights on?

00:13:39.650 --> 00:13:42.110
And the fifth one is
partnering with Uber

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:44.940
to ensure that everyone
is set up for success.

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:47.270
So, for example, after
the migration is done,

00:13:47.270 --> 00:13:49.610
what kind of observability
and introspection

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:50.570
do we need in place?

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:52.560
What kind of alerting
do we need in place?

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:55.280
The data protection
strategy, and the playbooks

00:13:55.280 --> 00:13:57.530
for incident management.

00:13:57.530 --> 00:14:00.320
The key was getting
aligned on a common goal,

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:03.710
and agreeing where we
want to be by what time,

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:06.250
and partnering closely
with Uber in this journey.

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:08.000
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
You're really giving us

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:09.890
a truly behind-the-scenes look.

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:12.770
Even I was curious
to know what happens,

00:14:12.770 --> 00:14:15.890
what needs to be done
to make this happen.

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:17.780
Any business with Uber scale--

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:20.690
obviously, no pun
intended-- will truly

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:24.390
benefit from just learning
what these steps were.

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:26.120
And it also
underscores that this

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:29.750
is possible to accomplish
a migration of such scale,

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:32.130
and how to actually
even get there.

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:34.160
So Ankit, I want
to ask you, what

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:36.500
are some of the
performance optimizations

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:40.917
that were done to deliver
this kind of user experience?

00:14:40.917 --> 00:14:42.500
ANKIT SRIVASTAVA:
As Pritam mentioned,

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:45.890
I think the more important part
was during the migration phase,

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:48.830
figuring out what is the right
amount of observability we need

00:14:48.830 --> 00:14:50.690
across the board
to ensure that we

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:52.640
can find these kind
of inefficiencies

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:54.770
quickly and iterate on them.

00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:58.550
This operational rigor, giving
attention to various dashboards

00:14:58.550 --> 00:15:01.970
metrics, and diligence in
evaluating every abnormality,

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:03.990
was extremely critical.

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:06.920
So some of the performance
optimizations that we worked on

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:08.990
in collaboration
across many teams

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:11.600
were primarily focused
on the networking stack.

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:16.130
The two primary issues that we
foresaw in the networking stack

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:19.490
was around the TCP resets
and intermittent packet loss.

00:15:19.490 --> 00:15:21.860
By optimizing the
gRPC channel pool,

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:24.830
whenever a channel is
affected by the TCP resets,

00:15:24.830 --> 00:15:27.320
the requests are automatically
forwarded temporarily

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:29.750
to a backup empty gRPC channel.

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:32.450
This allows for the request
to be forward instantly,

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:35.120
without having to wait
for a new Spanner session.

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:37.400
In a contained environment,
we saw improvements

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:42.110
of 1.7 times to 7 times the
average 2.5 time reduction

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:43.620
of errors.

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:47.600
Similarly, another common cause
of suboptimal availability

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:50.090
guarantee is
intermittent packet loss.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.220
Recovering from these
conditions can sometimes

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:54.500
be in order of minutes,
further degrading

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:56.865
the overall application
performance.

00:15:56.865 --> 00:15:58.760
With frequent
health checks we can

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:01.790
detect broken TCP connections
within five seconds,

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:03.530
and initiate a
graceful reconnect

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:05.450
on broken gRPC channels.

00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:08.570
With these improvements, we were
able to reduce the total number

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:11.108
of errors by almost four times.

00:16:11.108 --> 00:16:13.400
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Wow, those
networking improvements--

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:17.660
it sounds like they were so
critical to this whole setup.

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:21.560
Now, Pritam, how do you
optimize for cost when

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:23.937
using Spanner at that scale?

00:16:23.937 --> 00:16:25.520
PRITAM SHAH: Yeah,
we did a few things

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:28.170
to optimize for cost for Uber.

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:31.650
So for example, we
optimized node utilization.

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:35.000
So in case of a
multi-region configuration,

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:38.360
a read can be issued to the
leader or to the replica.

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:41.090
If a strong read is
issued to the replica,

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:43.700
it incurs the extra
cost of latency

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:45.830
until the replica has
the latest snapshot

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:47.840
to serve the strong read.

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:52.340
To reduce the impact on latency,
we leveraged Google's Private

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:56.000
Service Connect and rerouted
the strong reads to the leader

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:58.730
region, and all the
stale reads were routed

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:00.710
to the replica-only region.

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:03.980
This helped us in maximizing
the utilization of the nodes

00:17:03.980 --> 00:17:07.130
across the leader and
the replica region.

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:09.470
There are other cool
things that Ankit and team

00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:13.762
have done on the latency
and cost optimization.

00:17:13.762 --> 00:17:14.720
ANKIT SRIVASTAVA: Yeah.

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:17.930
In addition to the Private
Service Connect, which

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:20.720
optimizes how the traffic
routing works across the leader

00:17:20.720 --> 00:17:26.089
and replica region, we also did
an autoscaler and on-prem cache

00:17:26.089 --> 00:17:29.100
to improve the overall
latency and cost.

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:31.620
As you're aware that Uber's
traffic pattern is variable

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:35.700
by the time of day and week, we
provision for maximum capacity

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:38.100
at all time based resources.

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:41.400
To take advantage of Cloud
Spanner's elasticity,

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:43.830
we built an autoscaler
that constantly [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:47.020
the number of nodes based
on CPU utilization target.

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:50.130
So as the traffic is variable
based on a constant CPU

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:53.850
utilization target, we're able
to achieve maximum elasticity

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:56.230
out of the Cloud
Spanner cluster.

00:17:56.230 --> 00:18:00.320
Similarly, most of our
workload is very read-heavy.

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:03.850
So we built an on-prem cache to
improve the latency and cost.

00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:06.460
To maintain the same
consistency as Spanner,

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:10.090
we only route the
stale reads to Spanner,

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:14.200
and we allow our cache to
serve snapshot isolation based

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:15.700
on Spanner's [INAUDIBLE] time.

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:18.010
We built a query
caching functionality

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:21.640
to serve just the stale reads
from cache that is hydrated

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:23.382
by strong reads and writes.

00:18:23.382 --> 00:18:24.340
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Wow.

00:18:24.340 --> 00:18:24.840
Awesome.

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:29.020
OK, so what I heard is
networking optimizations,

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:32.590
caching, and also autoscaling.

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:33.850
Well, awesome.

00:18:33.850 --> 00:18:36.850
Ankit, Pritam, thank
you so much for sharing

00:18:36.850 --> 00:18:40.420
such a complex system design,
architecture, and migration

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:43.450
story with Cloud
Spanner as a star.

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:45.430
I am absolutely
blown away seeing

00:18:45.430 --> 00:18:49.330
what it takes for my Uber
ride to show up reliably.

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:52.210
PRITAM SHAH: It was absolutely
a pleasure partnering with Uber

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:55.510
on building the
fulfillment platform, which

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:58.540
